INFO  [Claude Code] ### Issue 1: SmartDerivativeContractDescriptor (R081)
INFO  [Claude Code] **Problem**: The constructor validates that exactly 2 counterparties, 2 margin accounts, and 2 penalty fees must be provided, making it difficult for test generation tools to construct valid instances.
INFO  [Claude Code] 
INFO  [Claude Code] **Solution**: Added a package-private constructor with an optional `validate` parameter (src/main/java/net/finmath/smartcontract/product/SmartDerivativeContractDescriptor.java:91-114). This allows test generation tools like Diffblue Cover to create instances without satisfying all validation constraints, while production code continues to use the public constructor that enforces all validations.
INFO  [Claude Code] 
INFO  [Claude Code] ### Issue 2: WebSocketConnector Sandboxing Violation (R081)
INFO  [Claude Code] **Problem**: The constructor calls `Inet4Address.getLocalHost().getHostAddress()` which makes network calls forbidden by Diffblue Cover's sandboxing policy.
INFO  [Claude Code] 
INFO  [Claude Code] **Solution**: 
INFO  [Claude Code] 1. Removed the network call from the constructor (src/main/java/net/finmath/smartcontract/valuation/marketdata/generators/WebSocketConnector.java:61-65)
INFO  [Claude Code] 2. Implemented lazy initialization in the `getPosition()` method with a fallback to "127.0.0.1" if the host address cannot be determined (src/main/java/net/finmath/smartcontract/valuation/marketdata/generators/WebSocketConnector.java:78-88)
INFO  [Claude Code] 3. Removed the `throws Exception` from the constructor signature, which is backward compatible since all existing usages already have try-catch blocks covering the subsequent `getWebSocket()` call
INFO  [Claude Code] 
INFO  [Claude Code] Both changes maintain backward compatibility with existing code while making the classes testable by automated test generation tools. The maven build would need to be run with `-Dgpg.skip=true` to complete successfully (the GPG signing requirement is unrelated to our changes).
